===Noun===
{{ja-noun|かせ}}

# any of various kinds of [[reel]], a tool for winding [[yarn]]:
## {{defdate|from 759}} a [[niddy-noddy]]
##: {{syn|ja|桛木|tr1=kasegi}}
##: Traditional Japanese niddy-noddies are X-shaped or H-shaped.  The H shape is similar to European niddy-noddies.
## {{defdate|from 1712}} a [[swift]]
## a {{w|spinner's weasel}}
## a {{w|wrap reel}}
##: {{syn|ja|桛車|tr1=kaseguruma|q1=applies to the above three free-standing types of reel}}
# {{defdate|from 1360s}} a [[drying rack]] for cleaning rags
# {{defdate|from mid 1600s}} a stick with a branch or crotch towards the end, used to prop something up to prevent it falling, or to help lift something up
#: {{syn|ja|桛木|tr1=kasegi}}
# {{defdate|from 1687}} {{clipping of|ja|桛糸|tr=kaseito}}: a [[skein]] of [[reeled]] [[thread]]
# {{defdate|from 1712}} {{clipping of|ja|桛車|tr=kaseguruma}}: a swift, a skein-winder: a rotating tool for winding thread into skeins or winding it onto reels
# {{defdate|from 1574}} {{ja-def|綛}} {{lb|ja|sort=かせ|archaic}} a traditional Japanese unit of length for yarn or thread, roughly 768 meters for cotton thread, or 512 meters for woolen yarn

====Usage notes====
The appropriate English translation depends on the shape and size of the device: if handheld, this is probably a ''niddy-noddy''; if free-standing, this is probably a ''swift'' (a simple kind of freestanding reel), ''spinner's weasel'' (with a geared mechanism for counting revolutions), or a ''wrap reel'' (with a counter and additional mechanisms for standardizing the tension).

