===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|y=k|うめ|alt=楳}}
[[File:広橋梅林 Hirohashi-bairin 2011.3.15 - panoramio.jpg|thumb|right|250px|{{m|ja||梅|tr=ume}}: '''Japanese plum trees''' and '''white plum blossoms''' of Hirohashi Bairin in Shimoichi, Nara]]
[[File:Japanese Crest Ume.svg|thumb|right|250px|{{m|ja||梅|tr=ume}}: a generic '''plum blossom''' crest]]
{{wp|ja:ウメ}}
{{wp|Prunus mume}}

{{IPAfont|⟨me<sub>2</sub>⟩}} → *{{IPAfont|/mːəɨ/}} → *{{IPAfont|⟨mume<sub>2</sub>⟩}} → {{IPAfont|⟨ume<sub>2</sub>⟩}} → *{{IPAfont|/uməɨ/}} → {{IPAfont|/ume/}}

From {{inh|ja|ojp|-}}.<ref name="KDJ">{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref><ref name="DJR"/><ref name="DJS">{{R:Daijisen}}</ref>

Probably ultimately from {{der|ja|ltc|-}} {{ltc-l|梅}},<ref name="DJR"/><ref name="DJS"/> with the borrowed ''me'' reading gaining a pronounced kind of initial ''m-'' sound, perhaps realized as *''mme''. The phonetic spelling was often rendered as {{m|ja||むめ|tr=*mme, mume}} from the {{w|Heian period}},<ref name="KDJ"/><ref name="DJS"/> with *''mme''/''mume'' and ''ume'' apparently existing in free variation. The reading eventually settled on {{m|ja||うめ|tr=ume}}. Compare the similar pattern of phonetic shift for {{m|ja|馬|tr=ma → *mma → muma → uma||[[horse]]}}, likely from {{cog|ltc|-}} {{ltc-l|馬}}.

